      "urpx:forRatePlan": {
        "@id": "gpc:pll"
      },
      "urpx:hasCoverageClaim": {
        "@id": "gpc:cc-pll-2026-06-01",
        "@type": "urpx:CoverageClaim",
        "urpx:completenessResult": {
          "@id": "urpx:partial"
        },
        "urpx:selfContained": true,
        "urpx:explainedBy": {
          "@type": "urpx:Evaluation",
          "@id": "gpc:cc-pll-2026-06-01-evaluation",
          "urpx:evaluationKind": {
            "@id": "urpx:coverageEvaluation"
          },
          "urpx:description": "This filing does not carry everything its source document states. 9 parts are deferred, listed here in the same words as the filing's tariff.toml [modeling].deferrals so the two records cannot drift. (1) Hours-use bands. The sheet prices kWh in bands bounded by hours times the Billing Demand: not greater than 200 hours (in blocks of kWh), 200 to 400 hours, 400 to 600 hours and over 600 hours. A urpx:ServiceBracket bound is a fixed number (https://github.com/urpx-org/urpx/issues/87), so each band's kWh is a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod, and the first band carries the kWh blocks; a consumer has to split the month's kWh by the Billing Demand. clj-urpx sums every energy ledger for a marginal rate, and the rider subtotals sum the same prices, so this filing's recorded marginal rate adds each higher band's price to the first block's, where the sheet prices a month's first kWh at the first block's price alone. (2) Billing Demand. Billing Demand is a calculated MetricInput on the 30-minute kW demand, with the sheet's ratchets over the current month and the preceding eleven, its floors and its rule for a customer changing from a time of use tariff stated in its description and formula. URPX v0.5.1 does not evaluate a calculationFormula, so a consumer has to compute it from twelve months of demand history, contract capacity and contract minimum. The sheet's seasons for it are billing months. (3) Fuel seasons by billing month. FCR-27 prices June through September and October through May, which Schedule R's bills show is read by the billing month a bill is rendered in. The JSON-LD gates the fuel PriceDefinitions on calendar months, so usage in the weeks a billing period runs ahead of its billing month takes the other season's fuel price. (4) Voltage level and franchise fee location. FCR-27's three voltage levels and MFF-11's two locations are crossed in one ProfileAlternatives set of six alternatives, because URPX v0.5.1 allows one set per PlanElements and the franchise fee's energy subtotal must include that voltage level's fuel prices. A consumer selects one alternative, for example Secondary Distribution inside city limits. (5) Municipal Franchise Fee base. MFF-11 applies to the total revenues of each bill, which include the ECCR-15 and DSM-C-15 amounts. Its multiplier Prices here are based on the energy-and-fuel subtotal and on the non-energy charges' Prices, not on the ECCR and DSM-C multiplier Prices, so the franchise fee on those two amounts, about 0.44 percent of the base bill inside city limits, is not carried. (6) Demand Side Management schedule. The sheet applies \"the Company's effective Demand Side Management Residential or Commercial Schedule\". DSM-C-15 applies to all Commercial Customers as the Company's Rules and Regulations for Electric Service define them, and is carried here as Prices with no condition, so it applies to every customer on the schedule. A Residential Customer served on the schedule would pay DSM-R-16, 1.1969 percent, instead; the sheet names no schedule for an Industrial Customer. Neither case is carried. (7) Excess Reactive Demand. Where a power factor below 95 percent lagging is indicated, the Company may install metering for reactive demand and bills kVAR above one-third of the measured kW at $0.43 per kVAR. The JSON-LD carries the $0.43 on a calculated MetricInput whose formula is text in its CalculationMethod; a consumer has to compute the excess kVAR, and supplies none for a customer the Company does not meter for it. (8) Minimum Monthly Bill. The sheet sets it at the $260.28 Basic Service Charge plus $13.86 per kW of Billing Demand, plus excess kVAR charges, ECCR, DSM, Fuel Cost Recovery on the current month's kWh and the Municipal Franchise Fee; for metered outdoor lighting, the lesser of that or the Basic Service Charge plus those riders. A minimum is a floor under the bill, which no Price expresses, so it is not carried; a modeled bill below it is low by the difference. (9) Limitation of Service, Construction Service and Term of Contract. Service is limited to customers whose calculated demand is 500 kW or greater (for customers on the Off-Peak and Variable Off-Peak riders, by the riders' percentages), and to customers with an expected total peak demand of 100 MW or greater only under contracts made before February 1, 2025; construction power is part of permanent service, with a payment in advance per metering point; the term is five years. The limitations are stated in the eligibility rule's description, and none of these is priced or carried.",
          "urpx:evaluatedOn": "2026-10-19T00:00:00"
        }
      }
    }
  }
